# Turns, in a wartime twin: rolling in, holding the turn, rolling out.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **This lesson names no speed**, which is why it exists for every class.
# `vref`, `stall` and `climb` come from an aeroplane's published figures, and
# only five of the sixteen publish a stall speed at all - the two Cessnas, the
# PA-28, the Learjet and the Mosquito. A turn is taught on bank and height,
# and both of those the simulation can see on any aeroplane.
#
# **The height is held against where the stage began**, not against a number:
# a turn is flown wherever you happen to be, and what the lesson teaches is
# that you come out of it at the height you went in at.
#
# **A left turn**, because a bank angle has a sign and a lesson has to pick
# one. `lesson/turned-deg` is how far she has turned since the stage began,
# which is what makes the measurement free of the wrap at north.

name Turns in a wartime twin
teaches second-world-war

stage Rolling into the turn
do Look out, then roll smoothly into the turn to the left
do Hold the nose up as the bank comes on: a turn needs more lift, not less
until attitude/phi-deg <= -20

stage Holding the turn
do Hold the bank steady and the nose where it was
do Keep the height: this is what the turn is for
hold attitude/phi-deg -45.0 -12.0 Hold the bank steady through the turn
hold position/h-agl-ft start-150 start+150 Hold your height through the turn
until lesson/turned-deg <= -60

stage Rolling out
do Roll the wings level and let the nose settle
until attitude/phi-deg >= -5
